\section{Helmet constructions}\label{sec:S5}

\subsection{The fixed adult helmet and its placements}\label{sec:S5.1}

We used the same Neuromag superconducting quantum interference device (SQUID) sensors and the same noise for every head. The centered placement puts each head's own head frame where the adult's lies in the sample recording (the recording's device-to-head transform), so that the ear line stays where the adult's was. The primary placement is top contact: the head is raised from the centered pose until the nearest magnetometer coil center is << F.g3b_cfg_clearance >> mm from the scalp, the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing plus a margin. This is a best-fit assumption rather than documented practice. A child in an adult helmet may be placed centrally and repositioned toward the expected source \citep{gaetz2008}, and a small child's shoulders can stop the head short of the top \citep{tesan2010}. Top contact therefore favors Neuromag, and the other placements bound this choice.

A family of << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> placements chosen from the geometry alone (source-blind) tests the sensitivity to placement; for child C, one lateral shift was infeasible, leaving << F.g3b_child_c_n_family_feasible >>. The family comprises top contact, back contact, shifts of ±<< F.g3b_cfg_translation >> mm left--right and front--back, and rotations of ±<< F.g3b_cfg_pitch >>° pitch, ±<< F.g3b_cfg_roll >>° roll and ±<< F.g3b_cfg_yaw >>° yaw, each raised to the same contact. Three further placements are the centered pose itself, a laterally centered pose raised to contact (the head moved sideways to the helmet's midline before the raise) and true contact at the Dewar spacing.

The adult's own $D$ depends on the placement: << F.g3b_adult_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at its measured pose, << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at top contact, << F.g3b_adult_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB laterally centered at contact and << F.g3b_adult_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at true contact. Over its << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements, the adult's $D$ spanned << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB (a spread of << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> dB), with top contact at rank << F.g3b_adult_top_rank_in_family >> from the lowest. In the fixed helmet, each smaller head was compared with the adult placed by the same rule. Their $\Delta$ was << F.g3b_smaller_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at the centered pose, << F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at top contact, << F.g3b_smaller_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB laterally centered at contact and << F.g3b_smaller_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at true contact.

With sensor plus brain noise, the point estimates had the same sign against the gradiometers (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_grad_ib_delta_range >> dB), against the magnetometers (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_mag_ib_delta_range >> dB) and for the site-matched array (<< F.g3b_smaller_matched_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB). With the room field projected out, $\Delta$ was unchanged in the scaled adults (<< F.g3b_scaled_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >> dB, against << F.g3b_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB) and lower in the templates (<< F.g3b_templates_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >> dB, against << F.g3b_templates_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB). In the children it was small: << F.g3b_child_a_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >> << F.g3b_child_a_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_ci >>, << F.g3b_child_b_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >> << F.g3b_child_b_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_ci >> and << F.g3b_child_c_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >> << F.g3b_child_c_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_ci >> dB for children A, B and C, child C's point estimate negative with an interval spanning zero. The site-matched arrays gave << F.g3b_child_a_matched_vs_combined_proj_delta >>, << F.g3b_child_b_matched_vs_combined_proj_delta >> and << F.g3b_child_c_matched_vs_combined_proj_delta >> dB, every interval including 0.

The adult's placement spread exceeded $\Delta$ in << F.g3b_smaller_delta_below_adult_family_span_count >> of the << F.g4_n_smaller_heads >> smaller heads. Each child's own $D$ varied by << F.g3b_child_a_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >>, << F.g3b_child_b_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> and << F.g3b_child_c_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> dB (A, B and C) over the same family. Against each head's family median instead of top contact, the difference of pooled medians from the adult shrank by << F.g3b_smaller_family_vs_top_shrink_range >> dB.

The optically pumped magnetometer (OPM) arrays were refitted to each head with the adult's rules: << F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> dense and << F.g3b_smaller_matched_sites_range >> site-matched sites on the smaller heads, at a median standoff of << F.g3b_all_dense_standoff_2dp_range >> mm above every head's own magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scalp. They are the same in every helmet. Sensor noise, room field and analysis band were unchanged. The cortical background variance per unit cortical area was calibrated once on the adult, so a smaller cortex carries less total background; as a sensitivity analysis, this variance was multiplied by << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_low >> and by << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_high >>.

In the realistic heads, fit and head size can act on $D$ through at least four routes. A wider SQUID gap and a shallower cortex would raise it, whereas fewer OPM sites and, in the school-aged children, a near-adult cortex close to the OPMs would lower it. The constructions below vary these routes together in different combinations, and none isolates one route (main text, Section 3.5). The last route rests on the children's unverified near-scalp anatomy (Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}).

\subsection{The helmet scaled with the head}\label{sec:S5.2}

Every coil center is scaled about the head origin by the child-to-adult ratio of head circumferences, keeping coil size, orientation and noise. The factor is raised in steps until no coil is within << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >> mm of the scalp. The helmet is thus scaled in proportion to the head, gaps included, unlike the constant gap of the sphere (Section~\ref{sec:S2}). It was computed about the centered head and, as a variant, about the laterally centered head; this variant was added after the first pediatric results were known, to remove the templates' lateral offset. The construction asks what the adult's fit would give at a child's size; it is not a model of a pediatric SQUID device. The main text treats it as a secondary construction, because it also brings the sensors closer than the adult's own fit (see below).

In the helmet scaled about the laterally centered head, $\Delta$ was << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB against the adult in its own scaled helmet. The highest upper interval bound, child C's, was << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci_hi_max >> dB (parcel-bootstrap intervals, which exclude placement and construction uncertainty). Only child C, whose scaled helmet keeps a median gap of << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_gap >> mm against the adult's << F.g3b_adult_cfx_gap >> mm, was not negative (<< F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> dB, an interval that also contains its fixed-helmet $\Delta$). With the room field projected out, the difference of medians from the adult in its own scaled helmet was << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_proj_d_minus_adult_cfx_range >> dB (<< F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_cfx_range >> dB with sensor plus brain noise).

Scaled about the centered head instead, the helmet left residual gains in the heads that sit off-center: the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month templates and child C (<< F.g3b_infant2yr_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> and << F.g3b_child_c_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> dB; $\Delta$ << F.g3b_smaller_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB overall). The OPM's absolute advantage remained in the scaled helmet ($D$ << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the smaller heads), but the additional child--adult contrast is no longer established.

Three checks qualify this result. First, part of the drop comes from the reference: against the adult at top contact rather than in its own scaled helmet, the difference of medians was << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_top_range >> dB (child C << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_top >> dB, above its fixed-helmet $\Delta$). Second, scaling the helmet also scales the gap, which shrank below the adult's << F.g3b_adult_cfx_gap >> mm in << F.g3b_cfx_gap_below_adult_count >> of the << F.g4_n_smaller_heads >> smaller heads (<< F.g3b_cfx_gap_below_adult_range >> mm). This helmet therefore fits most smaller heads more tightly than the adult's helmet fits the adult, which favors Neuromag; the helmet fitted at the adult's gap (Section~\ref{sec:S5.3}) avoids this confound. Third, the smaller heads carry fewer OPM sites (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> against << F.g3b_adult_dense_sites >>). The adult's array subsampled to those counts lost << F.g3b_smaller_site_penalty_vs_combined_range >> dB, so at an equal OPM site count $\Delta$ in the fixed helmet would be << F.g3b_smaller_eqcount_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB.

\subsection{The helmet fitted at the adult's gap}\label{sec:S5.3}

The Neuromag helmet is scaled about the head origin of each head's laterally centered pose until the median gap from magnetometer coil center to scalp equals the adult's. We used two targets: << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >> mm, the adult's median gap in its own helmet about its laterally centered head (scale factor << F.rev_cgap_adult_fitted_k >>), and, as a variant, << F.rev_cgap_adult_top_gap_mm >> mm, the adult's gap at top contact in the fixed helmet. Coil size, orientation, integration points and noise are kept. No coil center may come within the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing of the scalp or lie inside the head. In child C, this clearance binds before the target is reached: the helmet stops at a gap of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_mm >> mm, << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_above_target_mm >> mm above the target, with a scale factor of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_k >> instead of the << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_k_at_target >> that the target would need.

The OPM arrays are those of the fixed-helmet analysis, unchanged. A within-head contrast, the median over a head's targets of the target-wise difference between $D$ at top contact in the fixed helmet and $D$ in the fitted helmet, is therefore a property of Neuromag alone and is the same whichever OPM array is compared. Table~\ref{tab:S5} gives the gaps and scale factors of every helmet per head. Intervals use << F.rev_cgap_n_boot_primary >> parcel-bootstrap resamples for the dense array in the fixed helmet and in the helmet fitted at << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >> mm, and << F.rev_cgap_n_boot_secondary >> elsewhere. This helmet is a geometric control at the adult's median gap, not at its gap distribution, placement or coverage, and it is not a pediatric SQUID system.

