The within-head contrast measures what Neuromag loses, in a given head, to the fit, gap and placement of the fixed helmet together. It was << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the scaled adults, << F.rev_cgap_templates_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the templates and << F.rev_cgap_children_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the children. The adult's own contrast was << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >> dB, because its laterally centered pose in its own helmet is looser than top contact. Child C's contrast was negative although its fixed-helmet gap (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fixed_gap_mm >> mm) and its clearance-bound fitted gap (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_mm >> mm) nearly coincide. The fitted construction itself is worth << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >> dB in the adult at an equal median gap, so this contrast is one of construction, not of gap.

The interaction, the smaller head's contrast minus the adult's, is the extra that the fixed helmet gives a smaller head beyond what it gives the adult. It was << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_range >> dB in the scaled adults, << F.rev_cgap_templates_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_range >> dB in the templates and << F.rev_cgap_children_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_range >> dB in the children. At every vertex of a scaled adult, the interaction equals $\Delta$(fixed) − $\Delta$(fitted), because the same vertex is compared in both helmets. The area-weighted medians of these quantities are not additive, so $\Delta$(fixed) − $\Delta$(fitted) − interaction leaves a residual (Table~\ref{tab:S6}). The within-head and interaction estimates are therefore reported in their own right rather than derived from the $\Delta$ values.

To equalize OPM site counts, the adult's dense array was subsampled by farthest-point sampling to each smaller head's dense site count (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> sites), with the adult in the same fitted helmet. At the adult's gap, $\Delta$ was then << F.rev_cgap_smaller_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB: << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the scaled adults, << F.rev_cgap_templates_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the templates and << F.rev_cgap_children_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the children (the intervals of children A and B include zero). At the adult's median gap and equal OPM site count, a smaller head's dense array thus gains a little. One possibility, which these controls do not test, is that its cortex lies closer to the OPMs. In the scaled adults and templates, the fewer sites take about that much back. In children A and B, whose intervals at equal OPM site count include zero, the fewer sites take back more, and what else lowers their $\Delta$ is not settled by these controls (the children are provisional examples; Sections~\ref{sec:S4.3} and \ref{sec:S5.5}).

As a measure of placement uncertainty, $\Delta$ in the fixed helmet was computed at each of the << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements, each head against the adult at the same placement, as a difference of the area-weighted medians (Table~\ref{tab:S7}). In the scaled adults, the band was << F.rev_cgap_scaled_band_dense_vs_combined_ib_min_range >> dB at its lowest and << F.rev_cgap_scaled_band_dense_vs_combined_ib_max_range >> dB at its highest; in the children, << F.rev_cgap_children_band_dense_vs_combined_ib_min_range >> dB at its lowest and << F.rev_cgap_children_band_dense_vs_combined_ib_max_range >> dB at its highest. It was positive at every placement in every head except child C. The crossed band, which pairs any feasible placement of the head with any placement of the adult, is wider and includes zero in the children. Top contact was not an extreme of any band (Table~\ref{tab:S7}, rank).

