\subsection{Depth matching}\label{sec:S5.4}

Depth is the route of head size that the sphere describes (Section~\ref{sec:S2}). The cortex of the templates and children is shallower than the adult's: area-weighted median depths were << F.g3b_templates_depth_wmedian_range >> mm (templates) and << F.g3b_children_depth_wmedian_range >> mm (children), against << F.g3b_adult_depth_wmedian >> mm. Of the cortical area, << F.g3b_templates_area_share_10_20mm_pct_range >> (templates) and << F.g3b_children_area_share_10_20mm_pct_range >> (children) lay << F.g3b_depth_span_10_20_mm >> mm below the scalp, against << F.g3b_adult_area_share_10_20mm_pct >> in the adult. In the fixed helmet, reweighting each head's targets to the adult's depth distribution lowered the difference of pooled medians from the adult. In the templates it fell from << F.g3b_templates_pooled_diff_range >> dB pooled to << F.g3b_templates_reweighted_diff_range >> dB reweighted, and in the children from << F.g3b_children_pooled_diff_range >> dB to << F.g3b_children_reweighted_diff_range >> dB.

Within depth strata, where $\Delta$ is the difference of the two heads' area-weighted medians, $\Delta$ was positive in every stratum of the scaled adults and templates (<< F.g3b_templates_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_depth_delta_range >> dB; Fig.~\ref{fig:S7}A). For the individual children, it was negative in << F.g3b_children_depth_10_30mm_delta_negative_count >> of the << F.g3b_children_depth_10_30mm_cells_n >> child-strata at << F.g3b_depth_span_10_30_mm >> mm depth (<< F.g3b_children_dense_vs_combined_ib_depth_10_30mm_delta_range >> dB). No interval there lay above zero, the largest lower bound being << F.g3b_children_depth_10_30mm_ci_lo_max >> dB, and $\Delta$ was positive deeper (<< F.g3b_children_dense_vs_combined_ib_depth_30_60mm_delta_range >> dB; Fig.~\ref{fig:S7}B). In this decomposition, the children's fixed-helmet gain is matched by their shallower cortex. The depths are measured from the scalp used, which is unverified near the scalp in the children (Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}). Measured from the MRI boundary instead, they would shift by << F.rev_qc_children_targets_depth_change_p50_mm_range >> mm in the children, << F.rev_qc_adult_targets_depth_change_p50_mm >> mm in the adult and << F.wr_qc_templates_targets_depth_change_p50_mm_range >> mm in the templates. The decomposition describes the model's surfaces, not a tested mechanism.

In the helmet fitted at the adult's gap, the children's strata were negative throughout the depths that hold most of their cortex. For child A, $\Delta$ at the adult's gap was << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_depth_10_15mm_delta >> dB at << F.wr_cgap_depth_10_15_label >> mm, << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_depth_20_25mm_delta >> dB at << F.wr_cgap_depth_20_25_label >> mm and << F.rev_cgap_child_a_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_depth_30_40mm_delta >> dB at << F.wr_cgap_depth_30_40_label >> mm, and its intervals included zero only from the << F.wr_cgap_depth_40_50_label >> mm stratum down. The two decompositions, fitting the helmet and matching depth, are not additive, and neither apportions the routes exactly. The fitted helmet is the main control because it matches the adult's median gap, although it changes the gap distribution, the placement and the coverage with it.

